## IndexNow (after every publish)

Once the push is live, run `python3 scripts/indexnow-submit.py blog/<slug>.html blog.html` (full network). Use `--all` after site-wide edits. Never delete the root key file `e0a3a7baa14dbc0cad914cbae8ff7381.txt`.

## Quick answer at the top (every new post)

Directly after the H1/byline, the first paragraph is 2–3 plain sentences that answer the search question outright (featured-snippet target), with the primary keyword included naturally. No throat-clearing before it. Format: `<p><strong>Quick answer:</strong> …</p>` as the first element inside `blog-post-content`. All existing posts have one (added Sep 27, 2026); edits to existing posts are fine when title, content, and answer still match.

## Post style rotation (keep voice; change shape)

Same sales-safe voice and moat facts. Do **not** make every daily post the same “Focus + headphones + sound bed + tactile reset” protocol.

**Rotate shapes** week to week (and within multi-post days). Check the last 3–5 `blogs.json` titles before picking:

1. **Situation protocol** — concrete scene how-to (cafe, study hall, bedtime, open office). Steps + Focus/sounds/tools. Example: `focus-noisy-cafe-iphone`.
2. **Buyer / trust decision** — commercial or trust filter people search before they download (privacy labels, no-account, subscription vs buy-once, feature worth-it checklists). Deeper explanation + checklist; product beat fits the trust angle. Example: `app-privacy-label-calm-app-iphone` (Sep 26, 2026 “new style” reference).
3. **Mind / habit / sensory** — regulation without a full acoustic stack (mind break without feed, habit stack, sensory exit plan, overstimulation self-regulation).

Rules:
- Prefer a **different shape** from yesterday unless the user asks to continue a series.
- Space **distinct search intents** — do not clone cafe/office/study Focus posts back-to-back.
- Buyer/trust posts still lead with Google intent; they are not Soft sell essays. Useful first, CTA quiet at the end.
- When the user asks for “today’s example” / “new style,” default toward buyer/trust or a clearly non-protocol angle unless they specify otherwise.

## Positioning moat (facts every post; wording must vary)

Same facts, **not** the same paragraph. Do not copy-paste a stock “moat” block from post to post.

- **Always include (once, naturally)** in the product section and/or quiet CTA:
  - **Buy once:** free download; Pro is a **$4.99 one-time** unlock; no subscriptions; no ads.
  - **App privacy:** collects no personal data / no in-app tracking (source: `privacy.html`). Do not overclaim — the **website** may use analytics.
- **Vary the lead** by topic: pricing when the post is about cost/cancel; privacy when trust/ADHD clutter fits; sensory/tactile when overload is the theme. Rephrase every time so archives do not read as identical CTAs.
- **Replacement framing is optional**, not mandatory. Use “tired of subscription wellness apps” / cancel-replace language only when the keyword and angle support it. Do not force Calm/Headspace comparisons into how-to or acoustic posts.
- Keep the guide useful first. Moat lines are one short product beat + one quiet end CTA — not the article.
- Rotate exit-intent **topics** over time (subscription fatigue, sensory overload, privacy/native Apple tools, cancel/replace) so posts do not cannibalize existing `/blog` URLs or `blogs.json` titles.

## Sales-safe, not salesy

- Never frame the app or its tools as ineffective or “probably won’t help.”
- Soften clinical honesty without killing conversion: general wellness tool (not medical treatment) → practical usefulness.
- Competitor mentions: neutral or favorable to our buy-once / Apple-only / tactile / no-ads positioning. Never praise competitors in a way that makes SFF look lesser.
- No hype, urgency, fake scarcity, emoji spam, or hard-sell language.
- CTA once near the end: free download → try tools → unlock Pro once ($4.99) if useful.

## Product facts (verified only)

- Apple devices only (iPhone/iPad); App Store; built in Swift/Xcode.
- Free download; Pro = one-time $4.99; no subscriptions; no ads.

## Sources (vary them — don’t recycle the same three)

**Problem to avoid:** past posts overused NIMH + NCCIH + Apple Support on almost every article. That looks thin and samey.

**Going forward (every new post):**
- Use **2–4 outbound sources**, and **rotate** — do not default to the same NIMH/NCCIH/Apple trio every day.
- Prefer **topic-fit** credible pages (sales-upward only: support calm, sleep hygiene, sensory regulation, ADHD context, Apple how-tos). Never link pages that say these tools don’t work or discourage wellness apps.
- Include **at least one source that is not** NIMH, NCCIH, or a generic Apple Support “playing audio” page when another authority fits better.
- Over a week of posts, sources should clearly differ; reusing one core link is fine if you add different companions.

**Credible pool (pick what fits the keyword):**
- **Federal / public health:** NIMH, NCCIH, CDC, NHLBI, NIDCD, MedlinePlus, FDA (only when relevant)
- **Research discoverability:** PubMed / NIH pages (summarize carefully; mixed results → practical usefulness, not “don’t try”)
- **ADHD / autism orgs:** CHADD, Autism Speaks resource pages, NHS or reputable clinic explainers that are supportive
- **Sleep / hearing:** Sleep Foundation (reputational judgment), NHLBI sleep pages, NIDCD, ASHA when acoustic
- **Apple product how-tos:** Apple Support *specific* to the topic (Focus, Screen Time, Lock Screen, Sound, Background Sounds) — rotate which article, don’t always use the same “play audio” URL
- **Universities / major clinics:** Cleveland Clinic, Mayo Clinic, Rochester/URMC, etc. when the explainer is balanced and sales-safe

Still: sales-upward only. If a study is mixed, acknowledge variation, then emphasize safe practical use.

